-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Projection Failures:** If point $x$ projects outside the image bounds of an input view, the pixel-aligned feature $\phi_i$ must be handled (e.g., zero-padding or masking) to prevent invalid data from influencing attention [S1].
-*   **Calibration Errors:** The method is noted to be robust to calibration errors due to the attention mechanism's ability to weigh relevant views, but extreme misalignments may still degrade performance [S1].
-*   **High-Resolution Cost:** Directly using high-resolution raw RGB values increases memory and computational cost. The source suggests a two-phase training strategy (low-res pre-training, high-res finetuning) to mitigate this [S1].
+### Parameters
+*   $d_q$: Query view direction.
+*   $\gamma(p_i)$: Colored encoding mapping raw RGB values to a higher dimensional space [S1].
+*   Typical values: Not given in the sources.
+
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Calibration Errors**: The transformer is designed to be robust to geometry inference and calibration errors in real-world multi-view setups by learning attention relationships rather than relying on strict geometric projection [S1].
+*   **High-Resolution Cost**: Directly using high-resolution images increases training time. The source suggests a two-phase approach: low-resolution large-scale pre-training and high-resolution fine-tuning [S1].
+*   **Feature Alignment**: Pixel-aligned features $\phi_i(x, I_i)$ require accurate camera intrinsics and extrinsics for projection. Misalignment can degrade performance, though attention helps mitigate this [S1].
